3.71.m:
3.352
Nara and Nārāyaṇa said:
First of all, in a charming place, any one among the vicinity of a lake, a river, a spring, a house, a garden, or a mountain peak, one should prepare a very level, delightful sacred diagram measuring a cubit.
In its middle, one should draw an eight-petaled lotus, and then divide it with the described four-sided, four-gated line.
Then in the middle of the lotus pericarp, one should meditate on a white hundred-petaled lotus.
Above its pericarp is the solar disc, above that the lunar disc, above that the fire-disc; in its middle one should ritually place the supreme Cosmic Person, incorporeal, odourless, tasteless, formless, without touch, soundless, omnipresent, ending in a prolated syllable, the Oṃkāra.
On the eastern petal of the lotus one should ritually place the syllable "A", Vāsudeva, and meditate on him as white-coloured; on the southern petal one should ritually place the syllable "Ā", Saṃkarṣaṇa, and meditate on him shining like a lotus petal;
on the western petal, on the syllable "Oṃ", one should ritually place Pradyumna, and meditate on him as yellow-coloured;
on the northern petal one should ritually place the syllable "A", Aniruddha, and meditate on him as black-coloured;
on the north-eastern petal one should ritually place Brahmā with "Tat", and meditate on him shining like a lotus petal;
one should meditate on him as equal to ten million suns;
on the north-western petal one should ritually place Varāha;
one should meditate on him as equal to crushed collyrium;
at the eastern gate one should ritually place Vainateya with these syllables: "Oṃ Keṃ Ṭeṃ Yeṃ";
and one should meditate on him as golden-coloured;
at the southern gate one should ritually place Sudarśana, the chariot-wheel of splendour;
one should meditate on it as having a diamond hub, a thousand spokes, and splendour equal to ten million suns;
at the western gate one should ritually place the Śārṅga bow;
one should meditate on it as resembling Indra's bow;
at the northern gate one should ritually place Aniruddha with "Sveṃ Vai Ṭheṃ Heṃ Theṃ";
one should meditate on him as having all colours;
in the intermediate directions one should ritually place the conch Pāñcajanya with "Veṃ Ṇeṃ Kṣeṃ Kṣeṃ";
one should meditate on it as the colour of the moon;
one should ritually place Śrī, the lotus, to the south with "Gheṃ Heṃ Bheṃ Heṃ";
one should meditate on her as white;
one should ritually place Puṣṭi within the lotus with "Geṃ Ḍeṃ Veṃ Śeṃ";
one should meditate on her as white;
one should ritually place the forest garland in front with "Heṃ Seṃ";
one should meditate on it with "Heṃ Seṃ" as multi-coloured;
in the north-east one should ritually place the Nandaka sword with "Oṃ Cit";
one should meditate on it as the colour of space;
in the south-east one should ritually place the armour with "Oṃ M";
one should meditate on it as multi-coloured;
one should ritually place the Śrīvatsa mark with "Oṃ Kṣeṃ Leṃ";
one should meditate on it as white;
in the north-west one should ritually place the Kaustubha jewel with "Oṃ Cheṃ Ṭheṃ", and meditate on it as a blazing sun;
then in the sacred diagram, in their respective directions on the outside, one should ritually place the lords of the directions with the syllables "Oṃ" and "Auṃ": Śakra and Agni, Yama, Nairṛta, Varuṇa, Pavana, Kubera, and in the north-east [Īśāna];
one should meditate on them according to their described forms;
with the twelve-syllable mantra ending with "Oṃ namo bhagavate vāsudevāya", one should offer water for arghya, water for the feet, water for sipping, ointment, flowers, a lamp, incense, honey-mixture, food-offering, fruit, and edibles to the Cosmic Person in the middle of the pericarp; or with the eight-syllable mantra, "Oṃ namo nārāyaṇāya";
[or] with this mantra: "Oṃ, victory to You, O Lotus-eyed One! Homage to You, O Creator of the Universe! / Homage be to You, O Hṛṣīkeśa, O Great Cosmic Person, O First-born!"
or with the Puruṣasūkta;
then one should worship the other deities seated on the petals individually with the stated mantras.
That man who worships Viṣṇu according to this rite / accomplishes all aims and indeed becomes one who has achieved his goal.
